re Search in the site Test Drive Contact Menu Home Products Digital Engineering Design Verification Design Verification Model and Product Consistency Webinar Digital Engineering Basics – Building the MBSE Product Mod... Menu Digital Engineering MBSE with GENESYS GENESYS Version Comparison Sidekick Model-Based Product Development Model-Based Wire Harness Design Design Verification Digital Thread Connected Manufacturing Downloads Digital Engineering White Paper Digital Engineering eBook Download Vitech MBSE Primer GENESYS Software Documentation Resources Request A Demo Webinars Digital Engineering Blogs GENESYS Software Download GENESYS Help & Support Sidekick Help & Support Vitech is now Zuken GENESYS What’s New in GENESYS GENESYS Version Comparison GENESYS Software Download GENESYS Software Documentation GENESYS Help & Support Sidekick Sidekick Help & Support Model and Product Consistency As product complexity grows, so does the risk of divergence and the need for design verification events. The product implementation may have strayed from the original product model specifications lowering the business value. The in-process design must be verified against the product model at each design phase to lower risk and maximize business value. Verification Events Design verification is built upon verification events that check the consistency before moving to the next phase. For instance, an architecture design review should be successfully completed before a detailed design. The review activity should be captured by digital conversations as part of the Digital Thread. Requirement Verification Product requirements have a time or design phase consideration. For instance, a weight requirement needs to be checked during the architecture phase, the design phase, and the release to the manufacturing phase. The requirement test may use a different method at each phase, but it must be done. Over Constrained Designs In some cases, requirements can not be met. For instance, a PCB subsystem may not be able to hit a cost constraint because of the functionality requirements. This would require the model owner to re-allocate more dollars to the PCB Subsystem or reduce functionality. Traceability As requirements are verified at each design phase, a record is created, providing traceability for each requirement. If a PCB subsystem or wire harness is too heavy or costs too much in the final product, a requirement verification history will provide traceability to where the model and implementation diverged. Model Relevance The product model tends to become disconnected from the development process and ultimately the product life cycle. As the model becomes disconnected so does the risk of divergence. Design verification gates keep the model relevant throughout the product life cycle. The result is delivering the intended product business value. Features and Benefits Business Value Maintaining business value throughout the design process is always challenging. Design decisions increasing variance will impact business value. Strong product compliance with the model maintains business value. Verification Events GENESYS supports Verification Events which can be assigned to process milestones. The event is comprised of verification requirements that must be passed to proceed. Verific"
